      "id": "https://www.livescience.com/archaeology/middle-east/125-000-year-old-poisoned-stone-points-discovered-on-shore-of-ancient-lake-in-saudi-arabia",
      "url": "https://www.livescience.com/archaeology/middle-east/125-000-year-old-poisoned-stone-points-discovered-on-shore-of-ancient-lake-in-saudi-arabia",
      "title": "Stone points found in Saudi Arabia suggest poisoned hunting weapons 125,000 years ago",
      "content_html": "Archaeologists found more than 5,500 stone artifacts at ‘Uruq Aljelibah, a site beside an ancient lake in what is now Saudi Arabia’s Empty Quarter. Chemical traces on several points suggest people may have used plant toxins in hunting about 125,000 years ago. Resin and plant fibers at the bases of some points, along with animal tissue near damaged tips, also indicate that people attached the stones to shafts and used them against large animals.\n\nThe lake once covered about 340 square kilometers and supported animals including hippos, buffalo and gazelles. The discovery suggests people lived and hunted in Arabia during a wet period, rather than simply crossing the peninsula. Researchers have not established whether people deliberately prepared the toxins or whether they were effective. The stone artifacts were collected from the surface rather than the dated lake layers, so the lake deposits provide a maximum age for the tools, not a direct date.",
