      "id": "https://newsmed.pl/medycyna/onkologia/12500506/rak-jajnika-test-krwi-wykrywa-zmiany-na-wczesnym-etapie.html",
      "url": "https://newsmed.pl/medycyna/onkologia/12500506/rak-jajnika-test-krwi-wykrywa-zmiany-na-wczesnym-etapie.html",
      "title": "Experimental blood test detects ovarian precancerous changes with 92% sensitivity",
      "content_html": "Researchers at California’s Hoag Family Cancer Institute say a blood test measuring the RNA molecule Tu-Stroma detected serous tubal intraepithelial carcinoma (STIC), a microscopic precancerous change in the fallopian tubes, before an invasive ovarian tumor develops. In a separate validation group, the test identified 92.3% of women with STIC and correctly classified 98.2% of healthy women, according to the international study published in Nature Medicine. The study focused on high-grade serous ovarian cancer, which can begin in the fallopian tubes and is difficult to detect at an early stage.\n\nThe test is not approved or available for routine care. Researchers analyzed previously collected samples from people whose diagnoses were already known, rather than screening women prospectively. They plan follow-up studies of women with and without inherited risk to determine how early Tu-Stroma appears and whether test results can inform clinical decisions.",
      "date_published": "2026-11-13T06:51:00+00:00",
      "authors": [
