    {
      "id": "https://www.rfi.fr/en/environment/20261112-africa-s-push-to-triple-adaptation-finance-left-off-official-cop31-agenda",
      "url": "https://www.rfi.fr/en/environment/20261112-africa-s-push-to-triple-adaptation-finance-left-off-official-cop31-agenda",
      "title": "African negotiators lose COP31 agenda bid for adaptation finance",
      "content_html": "African negotiators failed to secure a dedicated negotiating track at the COP31 climate summit for their proposal to triple adaptation finance after wealthy countries opposed the request. The African Group of Negotiators (AGN), representing the continent’s 54 countries, is now seeking to give the target greater prominence in the summit’s final political declaration.\n\nThe dispute centers on a pledge made at COP30 to at least triple adaptation finance by 2035. Without a separate negotiating track, there will be no formal process to set a baseline, monitor progress or enforce the pledge. AGN chair Dr. Antwi-Boasiako Amoah says negotiators still need clarity on the funding baseline and a timeline for delivery.\n\nSeparately, six U.N. agencies launched a disaster-risk financing platform in Antalya, aiming to secure more than $3 billion to protect 75 million people and their livelihoods from climate shocks by 2030. COP31 talks continue through Nov. 20.",
      "date_published": "2026-11-12T06:23:49+00:00",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "rfi.fr",
          "url": "https://www.rfi.fr"
        }
